(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-12-02"] = {"chem-12-02-m01":{"idea":"Molality uses the mass of solvent, and mass does not change with temperature.","steps":["Molality m = moles of solute / kg of solvent.","The denominator is a mass of solvent, not a volume.","Mass stays the same when temperature changes.","So molality does not change with temperature."],"others":"Per litre of solution describes molarity, and a volume does change with temperature. The choice that says per kilogram of solvent but changes with temperature is wrong because mass is fixed.","trap":"Molality = kg of solvent (fixed). Molarity = litres of solution (changes).","answer":"per kilogram of solvent, and it does not change with temperature"},"chem-12-02-m02":{"idea":"Molarity has a volume in the denominator, and volume depends on temperature.","steps":["Molarity M = moles of solute / litres of solution.","Moles of solute stay the same on heating or cooling.","The volume of the liquid expands or shrinks.","So molarity changes with temperature."],"others":"The moles of solute, the mass of solute and the molar mass are fixed properties and do not change with temperature.","answer":"the volume of the solution changes with temperature"},"chem-12-02-m03":{"idea":"Mole fractions are parts of a whole.","steps":["x_A = n_A / (n_A + n_B) and x_B = n_B / (n_A + n_B).","Add them: x_A + x_B = (n_A + n_B)/(n_A + n_B) = 1.","So the sum is always 1."],"others":"The product x_A × x_B is never 1 for two parts. The difference being 1 would need x_B to be negative. The sum 100 is the total of percentages, not fractions.","answer":"x_A + x_B = 1"},"chem-12-02-m04":{"idea":"Henry's law links gas solubility to pressure.","steps":["Henry's law: S = k_H × p.","S is the solubility and p is the pressure of the gas.","So the solubility is directly proportional to pressure.","Soda is bottled under high pressure to dissolve more CO₂."],"others":"Not depending on pressure, being inversely proportional, or depending on the square are not what Henry's law says.","answer":"it is directly proportional to the pressure"},"chem-12-02-m05":{"idea":"Gas solubility in liquids falls as the temperature rises.","steps":["Dissolving a gas in a liquid gives out heat (exothermic).","Gas + liquid ⇌ solution + heat.","Heating pushes the equilibrium left, as Le Chatelier's principle says.","So less gas stays dissolved."],"others":"It does not increase on heating. It does not stay the same. A rise and then a fall has no basis for oxygen in water.","trap":"Warm water holds less oxygen; fish struggle.","answer":"decreases"},"chem-12-02-m06":{"idea":"Raoult's law gives the partial pressure of each volatile component.","steps":["The partial pressure of A is proportional to its mole fraction in the liquid.","The constant is the vapour pressure of pure A, p_A°.","p_A = x_A p_A°.","Total pressure: p = p_A + p_B."],"others":"Adding x_A and p_A° is wrong as the law is a product. Dividing p_A° by x_A would give a pressure higher than the pure liquid's. Using x_B with p_A° mixes two different components.","answer":"p_A = x_A p_A°, where p_A° is the vapour pressure of pure A"},"chem-12-02-m07":{"idea":"Relative lowering of vapour pressure is a ratio that depends on the amount of solute.","steps":["Raoult's law for the solvent: p = x_solvent p°.","(p° − p)/p° = 1 − x_solvent.","Since x_solvent + x_solute = 1, this is x_solute.","So it equals the mole fraction of the solute."],"others":"Molality and molarity are not the quantities in Raoult's law, which uses mole fractions. The mole fraction of the solvent is the ratio p/p°, not the lowering.","answer":"the mole fraction of the solute"},"chem-12-02-m08":{"idea":"An ideal solution follows Raoult's law fully because unlike molecules interact like like ones.","steps":["Forces between A–B equal those in A–A and B–B.","So the mixing gives no heat change: ΔH_mix = 0.","Also no volume change: ΔV_mix = 0.","It obeys Raoult's law at all concentrations."],"others":"A large volume change or a large heat change on mixing shows a non-ideal solution. Henry's law is about gas solubility, not the definition of an ideal solution.","answer":"obeys Raoult's law at all concentrations, with no heat change and no volume change on mixing"},"chem-12-02-m09":{"idea":"Positive deviation means the vapour pressure is higher than Raoult's law predicts.","steps":["Molecules escape more easily if they are held less strongly.","This happens when A–B attractions are weaker than A–A and B–B.","The vapour pressure rises above the ideal value.","Mixing is endothermic, and volume increases."],"others":"Equal attractions describe an ideal solution. Stronger A–B attractions give negative deviation. A non-volatile solute is a different case and not a deviation type.","trap":"Weaker A–B forces: positive deviation. Stronger A–B forces: negative deviation.","answer":"the A–B attractions are weaker than the A–A and B–B attractions"},"chem-12-02-m10":{"idea":"Acetone and chloroform attract each other strongly.","steps":["The H of CHCl₃ forms a hydrogen bond with the O of acetone.","A–B attraction is stronger than A–A and B–B.","Molecules escape less, so vapour pressure is lower than predicted.","That is negative deviation, and heat is given out on mixing."],"others":"They attract, not repel. Their vapour pressures are not identical, and that would not cause deviation. Absorbing heat on mixing goes with positive deviation.","answer":"hydrogen bonding between the two kinds of molecule makes A–B attractions stronger"},"chem-12-02-m11":{"idea":"An azeotrope is a mixture that behaves like a pure liquid on boiling.","steps":["At the azeotropic composition the vapour has the same composition as the liquid.","The mixture boils at one constant temperature.","So distillation cannot separate it further."],"others":"Simple fractional distillation cannot separate an azeotrope. Boiling over a wide range describes an ordinary mixture. Freezing at a constant temperature and boiling over a range is not what an azeotrope does.","answer":"boils at a constant temperature and gives a vapour of the same composition as the liquid"},"chem-12-02-m12":{"idea":"Colligative properties depend on counting particles.","steps":["Vapour pressure lowering, boiling point elevation, freezing point depression and osmotic pressure are colligative.","They depend only on how many solute particles are present in the solvent.","They do not depend on what the particles are."],"others":"Size, colour, density and nature of the solute are not what these properties depend on.","answer":"the number of solute particles, and not on their nature"},"chem-12-02-m13":{"idea":"Boiling point elevation is proportional to molality.","steps":["ΔT_b is directly proportional to m.","The constant is K_b, the molal elevation constant.","ΔT_b = K_b m.","For water, K_b = 0.52 K kg/mol."],"others":"K_b/m and m/K_b make ΔT_b fall as m grows, which is wrong. K_b m² has the wrong power of m.","answer":"ΔT_b = K_b m"},"chem-12-02-m14":{"idea":"Freezing point depression is proportional to molality.","steps":["ΔT_f is directly proportional to m.","The constant is K_f, the molal depression constant.","ΔT_f = K_f m.","For water, K_f = 1.86 K kg/mol."],"others":"Squaring m, adding K_f and m, or dividing K_f by m do not give a proportional link with units of kelvin.","answer":"ΔT_f = K_f m"},"chem-12-02-m15":{"idea":"Salt changes the freezing point of water.","steps":["Salt dissolves in the thin water layer on the ice.","A solution freezes at a lower temperature than pure water.","So the ice melts even if the air is below 0 °C."],"others":"Salt does not give out heat by reacting with ice. It does not work by raising the boiling point, and it does not increase the vapour pressure of ice.","answer":"it lowers the freezing point of water, so the ice melts"},"chem-12-02-m16":{"idea":"Osmotic pressure follows the same pattern as the ideal gas law.","steps":["The ideal gas law is PV = nRT, or P = (n/V)RT.","Replace n/V with concentration C and P with π.","So π = CRT.","This is the van't Hoff equation."],"others":"RT/C, C/RT and CR/T all have the wrong dependence. π must grow with both C and T.","answer":"π = CRT"},"chem-12-02-m17":{"idea":"Name solutions by comparing their osmotic pressures.","steps":["Isotonic means equal osmotic pressure.","With a semipermeable membrane between them, there is no net solvent flow.","0.9% NaCl is isotonic with blood."],"others":"Hypertonic is higher osmotic pressure and hypotonic is lower. Ideal solutions follow Raoult's law, a different idea.","trap":"Iso = equal.","answer":"isotonic solutions"},"chem-12-02-m18":{"idea":"Reverse osmosis forces solvent to move against its natural direction.","steps":["Natural osmosis moves water towards the concentrated solution.","If a pressure larger than π is put on the solution side, the flow reverses.","Pure water passes through the membrane to the solvent side."],"others":"Pressure on the pure solvent side would not push water out of the solution. A smaller pressure than π cannot reverse the flow. Heating above the boiling point is distillation.","answer":"a pressure larger than the osmotic pressure is applied on the solution side"},"chem-12-02-m19":{"idea":"The van't Hoff factor i compares the real number of particles with the expected number.","steps":["i = observed effect / calculated effect without any change of particles.","Glucose neither splits nor joins in water.","The particle number is unchanged.","So i = 1."],"others":"0.5 is for association into dimers. 2 is for a solute that gives two ions. 0 is not possible for a solute that dissolves.","answer":"1"},"chem-12-02-m20":{"idea":"Dimer formation reduces the number of particles.","steps":["Two acetic acid molecules join into one dimer.","The number of particles in solution is about half.","So the colligative effect is about half and i ≈ 0.5."],"others":"i ≈ 2 or 3 is for dissociation into ions. i ≈ 1 is for no change. Dimer formation makes i less than 1.","answer":"about 0.5"},"chem-12-02-m21":{"idea":"The solution with the most particles has the lowest freezing point.","steps":["ΔT_f = i K_f m, and all four have the same m.","Assume full dissociation: AlCl₃ gives 4 ions (i = 4), K₂SO₄ gives 3, NaCl gives 2, glucose gives 1.","AlCl₃ has the largest i, so the largest ΔT_f.","So it has the lowest freezing point."],"others":"K₂SO₄ gives 3 particles, NaCl gives 2 and glucose gives 1, so each lowers the freezing point less than AlCl₃.","answer":"AlCl₃"},"chem-12-02-m22":{"idea":"Osmotic pressure depends on the number of dissolved particles.","steps":["π = iCRT.","Both solutions are 0.1 M at the same temperature.","NaCl splits into Na⁺ and Cl⁻, so i ≈ 2.","Sucrose stays whole, so i = 1.","NaCl has twice the particles and the higher π."],"others":"Size of molecules does not matter. Not dissociating makes sucrose lower, not higher. Equal concentration does not mean equal particles, since NaCl gives about twice as many.","answer":"0.1 M NaCl, because it gives about twice as many particles"},"chem-12-02-m23":{"idea":"A semipermeable membrane is selective.","steps":["Small solvent molecules, such as water, pass through.","Larger solute molecules or ions do not.","Osmosis uses this to move solvent from dilute to concentrated solution."],"others":"Letting solute but not solvent pass is the reverse. Passing neither would block osmosis. Letting both pass freely is not selective.","answer":"the solvent molecules to pass through but not the solute molecules"},"chem-12-02-m24":{"idea":"Water moves by osmosis from lower to higher solute concentration.","steps":["The outside solution is more concentrated than the cell fluid.","The cell membrane is semipermeable and lets water pass.","Water flows out of the cell towards the stronger solution.","So the cell shrinks."],"others":"Swelling happens when the outside is more dilute. Salt does not freely cross the membrane. The membrane is not sealed, because water can pass.","answer":"shrink, because water flows out of the cell"},"chem-12-02-m25":{"idea":"Colligative properties depend on the number of particles, not their identity.","steps":["Osmotic pressure, vapour pressure lowering and boiling point elevation are colligative.","Density depends on the mass and nature of the solute.","So density is the exception."],"others":"The other three are the standard colligative properties.","trap":"Read NOT carefully.","answer":"the density of the solution"},"chem-12-02-m26":{"idea":"Mass percentage uses the mass of the whole solution.","steps":["Mass of solution = 5 + 95 = 100 g.","Mass % = (5 / 100) × 100.","So the mass percentage is 5%."],"others":"95% is the solvent percentage. 5.26% divides by the solvent mass only. 19% is 95/5 or a similar slip.","trap":"Divide by the total solution mass, not the solvent.","answer":"5%"},"chem-12-02-m27":{"idea":"Mole fraction is the part divided by the total moles.","steps":["Total moles = 1 + 9 = 10.","x(solute) = 1 / 10.","So the mole fraction is 0.1."],"others":"0.9 is the mole fraction of the solvent. 10 is the total moles, not a fraction. 0.111111 is 1/9, which divides by the solvent only.","answer":"0.1"},"chem-12-02-n01":{"idea":"Molarity is moles of solute per litre of solution.","steps":["Moles of NaOH = 4 / 40 = 0.1 mol.","Volume = 500 mL = 0.5 L.","M = 0.1 / 0.5 = 0.20 mol/L."],"trap":"Convert mL to L before dividing.","answer":"0.20 mol/L"},"chem-12-02-n02":{"idea":"Molality is moles of solute per kilogram of solvent.","steps":["Moles of glucose = 18 / 180 = 0.1 mol.","Mass of water = 250 g = 0.25 kg.","m = 0.1 / 0.25 = 0.40 mol/kg."],"trap":"Use the solvent mass, not the solution mass, and change g to kg.","answer":"0.40 mol/kg"},"chem-12-02-n03":{"idea":"Find moles of each, then take water over total.","steps":["n(water) = 36 / 18 = 2 mol.","n(ethanol) = 46 / 46 = 1 mol.","x(water) = 2 / (2 + 1) = 2/3.","= 0.667."],"trap":"Check: x(water) + x(ethanol) = 0.667 + 0.333 = 1.","answer":"0.667"},"chem-12-02-n04":{"idea":"Use Raoult's law for the solvent: p = x_solvent p°.","steps":["x(solute) = 0.1, so x(solvent) = 0.9.","p = 0.9 × 760.","p = 684 mmHg."],"trap":"The pressure falls from 760, so the answer must be less than 760. The lowering is 76 mmHg.","answer":"684 mmHg"},"chem-12-02-n05":{"idea":"Use ΔT_b = K_b m. Glucose does not dissociate, so i = 1.","steps":["K_b = 0.52 K kg/mol and m = 0.5 mol/kg.","ΔT_b = 0.52 × 0.5.","ΔT_b = 0.26 K."],"trap":"The boiling point becomes 100.26 °C.","answer":"0.26 K"},"chem-12-02-n06":{"idea":"First find molality, then use ΔT_f = K_f m. Urea does not dissociate.","steps":["Moles of urea = 3 / 60 = 0.05 mol.","Mass of water = 100 g = 0.1 kg, so m = 0.05 / 0.1 = 0.5 mol/kg.","ΔT_f = 1.86 × 0.5.","ΔT_f = 0.93 K."],"answer":"0.93 K"},"chem-12-02-n07":{"idea":"Use the van't Hoff equation π = CRT.","steps":["C = 0.01 mol/L, R = 0.0821 L atm/(mol K), T = 300 K.","π = 0.01 × 0.0821 × 300.","π = 0.246 atm."],"trap":"Temperature must be in kelvin.","answer":"0.246 atm"},"chem-12-02-n08":{"idea":"Use ΔT_f = K_f m to get molality, then convert to molar mass.","steps":["m = ΔT_f / K_f = 0.64 / 5.12 = 0.125 mol/kg.","Moles = m × kg of solvent = 0.125 × 0.050 = 0.00625 mol.","Molar mass = mass / moles = 1.5 / 0.00625.","= 240 g/mol."],"trap":"Convert 50 g of benzene to 0.050 kg.","answer":"240 g/mol"},"chem-12-02-n09":{"idea":"NaCl splits into two ions, so the van't Hoff factor is 2.","steps":["NaCl → Na⁺ + Cl⁻, so i = 2.","ΔT_f = i K_f m = 2 × 1.86 × 0.1.","ΔT_f = 0.372 K."],"trap":"Twice the value for a non-electrolyte of the same molality (0.186 K).","answer":"0.372 K"},"chem-12-02-n10":{"idea":"Use Henry's law: solubility is proportional to pressure.","steps":["S ∝ p, so S₂ / S₁ = p₂ / p₁.","S₂ = 0.0014 × (3 / 1).","S₂ = 0.0042 mol/L."],"trap":"Three times the pressure gives three times the solubility.","answer":"0.0042 mol/L"}};
